{
  "_note": "Which party held the majority of each chamber, by date. A committed reference table, not derived from terms.json: roster counts include mid-Congress replacements and caucusing independents, so a raw count can mislabel the majority (e.g. the 110th Senate is 51-51 on the roster). `from`/`to` are inclusive ISO dates; `to` null = current. Every chamber's rows are contiguous from 1933-03-04 (the 73rd Congress; the Laws page clips to its own 1973 start, and the Economy and Trade charts to theirs). `seats` gives each Congress's party seat counts at its start (Democrats, Republicans, other; Senate independents counted with the party they caucused with), for the 73rd to the 119th; the majority rows above, not the counts, say who held control. Validated against roster counts (loosely) by lib/congress-control.test.ts.",
  "sources": {
    "senate": "U.S. Senate, Party Division in the Senate, 1789-Present, https://www.senate.gov/history/partydiv.htm (control by date, incl. the 1981 and 1987 changes of majority and the 2001 and 2021 mid-Congress changes; 1973-93 re-read 2026-10-08, no majority changed hands mid-Congress); 1933-73 and the seat counts added 2026-10-16 from the same page",
    "house": "U.S. House of Representatives, History, Art & Archives, Party Divisions of the House of Representatives, https://history.house.gov/Institution/Party-Divisions/Party-Divisions/ (fetched 2026-10-01; 1973-95 re-read 2026-10-08: the Democrats held a majority of seats in every Congress from the 93rd to the 103rd); 1933-73 and the seat counts added 2026-10-16 (Democrats held both chambers in every Congress from the 73rd to the 92nd except the 80th and 83rd)"
  },
